WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.682
This slide turns the join types into business-language decisions.

00:00:03.682 --> 00:00:08.512
If the question is only about patients who have admissions, then a match is required,

00:00:08.512 --> 00:00:10.552
so INNER JOIN is appropriate.

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:15.272
If the question says every patient, with admission information when available,

00:00:15.272 --> 00:00:21.652
then the patient population must be preserved, which points to LEFT JOIN when patients is on the left.

00:00:21.652 --> 00:00:29.577
If the question says every department and staff when available, we can preserve departments with RIGHT JOIN if

00:00:29.577 --> 00:00:32.907
departments is on the right, or we can reorder the tables and use LEFT JOIN.

00:00:32.907 --> 00:00:39.242
The result can be logically equivalent, but the reordered LEFT JOIN is often easier to read.

00:00:39.242 --> 00:00:44.107
If the goal is to compare two systems and show unmatched records from either side,

00:00:44.107 --> 00:00:46.582
FULL OUTER JOIN is a natural choice.

00:00:46.582 --> 00:00:52.472
Pay attention to words such as only matched, every, including those with none,

00:00:52.472 --> 00:00:54.807
and unmatched from either source.

00:00:54.807 --> 00:00:59.291
Those phrases tell you more about the correct join than memorizing a diagram.
